---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';

const comparison = [
  { platform: 'Decide what you should see', tool: 'Give you access to everything' },
  { platform: 'Optimize for engagement', tool: 'Optimize for understanding' },
  { platform: 'Represent your interests to you', tool: 'Help you understand your interests' },
  { platform: "You're the product", tool: "You're the owner" },
  { platform: 'Delegate your sovereignty', tool: 'Enhance your sovereignty' },
];

const directAccessExamples = [
  {
    title: 'For ballot propositions',
    description: 'A 47-page ballot measure becomes a plain-language summary with fiscal impact analysis and arguments for and against — generated by AI running on the node serving your community, not filtered by an algorithm optimizing for engagement. The full source text is always one click away.',
  },
  {
    title: 'For campaign finance',
    description: 'See exactly who funded an initiative, how the money was spent, and what outside groups are involved. Aggregated from public-domain databases into a single view — not curated by a platform deciding what spending is "newsworthy."',
  },
  {
    title: 'For government meetings',
    description: 'Every public legislative meeting in your jurisdiction, searchable by body, with agendas, minutes, and video links. Not curated. Not filtered. Available.',
  },
  {
    title: 'For your representatives',
    description: 'Enter your address. See every elected official who represents you — with bios, committee assignments, and a built-in contact form. No tracking of which representatives you look up or what you write to them.',
  },
];

// Live, maintained sources only. GovTrack's bulk data and API ended in 2017 and
// ProPublica's Congress API shut down on 10 July 2024; both were previously listed
// here as current. Do not re-add a source without checking it still publishes.
const academicSources = [
  {
    title: 'Legislative Effectiveness Score',
    source: 'Volden & Wiseman — UVA / Vanderbilt',
    description: '15 indicators tracking every bill a member sponsors from introduction through enactment, weighted by significance. The Center for Effective Lawmaking has extended the methodology to state legislatures — directly applicable to every state node.',
  },
  {
    title: 'DW-NOMINATE',
    source: 'Poole & Rosenthal',
    description: 'The academic standard for classifying legislative voting behavior across the left-right spectrum. Decades of methodological refinement and peer review.',
  },
];

// The limits of the argument, stated on the page rather than left implicit.
const concessions = [
  {
    title: 'Ten percent qualifies a measure. It does not pass one.',
    body: 'Winning a majority at the ballot is a separate and far larger problem. Nothing about a signature threshold addresses it.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Cheaper knowledge is not a motive to care.',
    body: 'We can lower what it costs to understand what is on your ballot. We cannot manufacture the desire to look. We have no evidence yet that the first produces the second.',
  },
  {
    title: 'The iron law applies to us.',
    body: 'Michels held that organization concentrates control even in movements founded to prevent exactly that. An organization built to disperse civic power will tend to accumulate it. Open code, a public AI charter, and a foundation rather than a company are mitigations. They are not immunity.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Cheaper organizing is cheaper for everyone.',
    body: 'A tool that lowers coordination costs lowers them for people whose aims we would find objectionable too. That is what neutrality costs, and we think it is the right price.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Why It Matters"
  description="An organized few decide and the disorganized many do not. Why that is a problem of justice, why the county is the scale where it can be answered, and the strongest argument that we are wrong."
>
  <Hero
    eyebrow="The Argument"
    title="Numbers are not power until they are"
    titleEm="organized."
    subtitle="In any large polity, an organized few decide and the disorganized many do not. This is not a claim about corruption or conspiracy. It is a claim about coordination costs, and unlike most claims in politics it can be checked against a statute."
    declaration="Under California Elections Code §9118, a petition signed by 10% of a county's gubernatorial vote compels its board of supervisors to act. In Alpine County that is roughly sixty people. A group that size already governs there, whether or not it is the people who live there."
    ctaText="See the Tools"
    ctaHref="/tools"
    secondaryCtaText="The Philosophical Foundation"
    secondaryCtaHref="/foundation"
  />

  <Section eyebrow="01 — Descriptive" title="The organized few">
    <div class="max-w-2xl space-y-5 text-ink/80 leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        Mosca's argument, made in 1896 and not seriously dislodged since, is that a minority governs
        not because it is wiser or better born, but because it is organized against a majority that
        is not. Numbers alone are inert. Coordination is what converts them into power.
      </p>
      <p>
        Schattschneider sharpened it sixty years later. The decisive power is not the vote but the
        prior question of which alternatives get considered at all, and the chorus of organized
        interests, he observed, sings with an upper-class accent. Whoever frames the ballot has won
        most of the argument before anyone votes.
      </p>
      <p class="font-medium text-ink">
        This is why a ballot explainer is a political intervention and not a public service.
      </p>
      <p>
        Tocqueville supplies the last piece, and it is the reason this site is organized around
        counties rather than states. Local institutions are where citizens actually learn the
        practice of liberty. The county is not a design convenience. It is the scale at which the
        practice is available to an ordinary person.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="02 — Normative" title="A liberty you cannot afford to use" background="gray">
    <div class="max-w-2xl space-y-5 text-ink/80 leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        Formal political equality is worth very little when the knowledge required to use it is
        distributed as unevenly as wealth is. Every citizen has the right to petition, to comment, to
        show up, to vote. Exercising those rights well requires time, research, and interpretive
        skill that are not distributed equally at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        Rawls gave this defect its name. The political liberties must have <strong class="text-ink">fair
        value</strong>, not merely formal existence. A liberty that some citizens lack the resources
        to exercise is a liberty in name only, and its worth has to be guaranteed rather than
        asserted. This is the strongest claim we make, because it converts the mission from an
        aspiration into an obligation.
      </p>
      <p>
        Dewey, writing in 1927 against Lippmann, located the failure in the same place. The public is
        not absent; it is eclipsed. It cannot recognize itself or trace which decisions affect it.
        The remedy is not better citizens but better conditions of inquiry and communication.
      </p>
      <p class="font-medium text-ink">
        This is not a complaint about apathy. It is a defect in the fair value of political liberty.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="03 — Remedial" title="Change the conditions, not the citizens">
    <div class="max-w-2xl space-y-5 text-ink/80 leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        The response is not to hand government to experts, and not to exhort citizens to care more.
        Both have been tried for a century. The response is to change the conditions under which
        citizens act: lower the cost of knowing what is on the ballot, who is paying for it, and what
        can be done about it, at the scale where one person's participation is not negligible.
      </p>
      <p>
        Paine is the ancestor here, more than any of the others. He wrote in deliberately plain
        language because comprehensibility was itself the political act; the pamphlet was
        simultaneously the argument and the distribution method. That is the standard this project
        holds itself to.
      </p>
      <p class="font-medium text-ink">
        That third move is the product. The first two are why it matters.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Tools vs. Platforms" background="gray">
    <div class="max-w-2xl mb-10 space-y-4 text-ink/80">
      <p>
        <strong class="text-ink">Platforms mediate.</strong> They stand between you and information, making choices about what you see. They represent your interests to you — based on what keeps you engaged, not what helps you understand.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong class="text-ink">Tools enable.</strong> They give you direct access to information and help you make sense of it yourself. They serve your agency rather than substituting for it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full max-w-2xl text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-ink/10">
            <th class="text-left py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-ink">Platforms</th>
            <th class="text-left py-3 font-medium text-ink">Tools</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {comparison.map(row => (
            <tr class="border-b border-ink/5">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-ink/70">{row.platform}</td>
              <td class="py-3 text-ink/80">{row.tool}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-8 text-ink/80 max-w-2xl">
      The difference isn't just philosophy — it's architecture. And architecture is a choice.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="The Objection" title="The strongest argument that we are wrong">
    <div class="max-w-2xl space-y-5 text-ink/80 leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        Anything built on the premise that better civic information matters has to answer the argument
        that it changes nothing. The best version of that argument is Anthony Downs's.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong class="text-ink">Rational ignorance.</strong> A single vote decides essentially
        nothing. The expected benefit of becoming informed is therefore near zero, while the cost in
        time and attention is entirely real. On this account, remaining ignorant is not a character
        failure. It is the correct decision, arrived at rationally. Publishing more information
        changes nobody's behavior, because cost was never the binding constraint. Pointlessness was.
      </p>
      <p>
        That argument is serious and it cannot be waved away. But notice its shape.
        Downs's conclusion rests on a product of two terms: the cost of knowing, multiplied by how
        little one's participation matters. Most civic technology attacks only the first term, which
        is why most civic technology fails on exactly Downs's prediction.
      </p>
      <p class="font-medium text-ink">
        The county scale attacks the second term, and attacks it harder.
      </p>
      <p>
        One signature among the 5,074 that compel Nevada County's board of supervisors is not a
        lottery ticket. It is roughly one part in five thousand of a binding outcome, several orders
        of magnitude removed from one vote among eleven million in a statewide race. Downs is right
        about the state. He is much weaker about the county, and the threshold for
        <a href="/thresholds" class="underline underline-offset-2 hover:opacity-70 transition-opacity">every one of California’s 58 counties</a>
        is published, sourced, and checkable.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is why the local threshold, rather than a statewide target, is the organizing device of
        this entire project. It is not a presentation choice. It is the load-bearing response to the
        strongest objection anyone has made to us.
      </p>
      <p>
        California is where we start, not the limit of the argument. Every state has bodies whose
        decisions turn on the attention of very few people, and most set the numbers that bind them
        by statute. The figures differ, the statutes differ, and in some states the citizen
        initiative does not exist at all. The method survives all of that: find the real number in
        your jurisdiction, publish it, and work at that scale.
      </p>
      <p>
        Lippmann and, in our own decade, Jason Brennan press a different line: that ordinary citizens
        cannot know enough to govern, so power should sit with those who can. Both are worth engaging
        rather than dismissing, and both are answered at length, along with Downs, in the
        <a href="/foundation" class="underline underline-offset-2 hover:opacity-70 transition-opacity">philosophical foundation</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="What we concede" background="gray">
    <div class="max-w-2xl mb-12 text-ink/80 leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        These are the limits of the argument. A case that only survives when its weaknesses are hidden
        is not a case worth making.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-x-12 gap-y-10">
      {concessions.map(c => (
        <div class="border-l-2 border-gold pl-6">
          <h3 class="text-base font-medium text-ink mb-2">{c.title}</h3>
          <p class="text-ink/80 text-sm leading-relaxed">{c.body}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <p class="mt-12 text-ink/80 max-w-2xl text-sm leading-relaxed">
      One more. <strong class="text-ink">Our position is falsifiable and has to stay that way.</strong> If civic knowledge becomes genuinely cheap and participation does not improve, Brennan gains ground and we should say so rather than move the goalposts.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section title="We don't grade your representative. Political scientists already do.">
    <div class="max-w-2xl space-y-5 text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-12">
      <p>
        A natural extension of mapping the financial relationships behind votes is asking how effectively a representative actually does the work of legislating. When we build it, we will not invent a proprietary scoring methodology for it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The academic standards already exist and have been peer-reviewed for decades. The plan is to aggregate what they already measure and add the layer none of them currently provide: <strong class="text-ink">what those scores mean for a specific constituent, in their district, on the issues they care about.</strong>
      </p>
    </div>

        <p class="text-sm bg-off rounded-lg border border-ink/10 p-4 max-w-2xl mb-12">
      <span class="font-medium text-ink">Status:</span> Effectiveness scoring is designed, not built.
      The sources below are the ones we intend to aggregate, not ones the tool reads today.
      <a href="/tools" class="underline underline-offset-2 hover:opacity-70 transition-opacity">The tools page</a>
      tracks what has actually shipped.
    </p>

    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-x-12 gap-y-10">
      {academicSources.map(source => (
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-base font-medium text-ink mb-1">{source.title}</h3>
          <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-dim mb-3">{source.source}</p>
          <p class="text-ink/80 text-sm leading-relaxed">{source.description}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <p class="mt-12 text-ink/80 max-w-2xl text-sm leading-relaxed">
      The tool is the interface. The authority belongs to the sources. The intent is to translate peer-reviewed political science into something a working voter can read in five minutes, linking back to the original methodology every time.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Direct access" background="gray">
    <div class="max-w-2xl space-y-6 text-ink/80">
      <p>
        Lowering the cost of knowing is not an abstraction. It is a set of specific things a person
        can do in an afternoon that currently take a professional researcher a week.
      </p>
      <p>
        Paine's insight was that the form of the argument and its distribution were the same problem.
        Making a 47-page measure legible is not a courtesy extended to citizens after the real
        political work is done. It <em>is</em> the political work.
      </p>
      <p class="font-medium text-ink">
        What does direct access look like?
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 mt-10">
      {directAccessExamples.map(example => (
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-medium mb-2">{example.title}</h3>
          <p class="text-ink/80 text-sm">{example.description}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <p class="mt-10 text-ink/80 max-w-2xl">
      No intermediary deciding what you should see. Just direct access to the information you need to make your own decisions. See <a href="/tools" class="underline underline-offset-2 hover:opacity-70 transition-opacity">what's built</a> and how our <a href="/transparency" class="underline underline-offset-2 hover:opacity-70 transition-opacity">AI stays neutral</a>.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section background="dark">
    <div class="max-w-xl">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4 tracking-tight">Build with us</h2>
      <p class="text-paper/70 mb-8">
        We're building civic tools that communities own, control, and shape. Not platforms that mediate. Infrastructure that enables.
      </p>
      <div class="flex gap-3">
        <a
          href="/get-involved"
          class="inline-flex items-center bg-gold text-ink font-semibold px-6 py-2.5 rounded-lg hover:brightness-95 transition-[filter] text-sm"
        >
          Get Involved
        </a>
        <a
          href="/foundation"
          class="inline-flex items-center text-paper/80 hover:text-paper px-6 py-2.5 text-sm font-medium transition-colors"
        >
          Read the Foundation
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-12 text-paper/50 text-xs max-w-xl">
      This page draws on the <a href="/foundation" class="underline hover:text-paper/70 transition-colors">philosophical foundation</a>, on Craig Volden and Alan Wiseman's Legislative Effectiveness Score, and on <a href="https://rodneygagnon.substack.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline hover:text-paper/70 transition-colors">Democracy Dialogues</a>, which explores how classical political philosophy illuminates our digital challenges.
    </p>
  </Section>
</BaseLayout>
